  "worked_solution": "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x=-1$ are **local maximums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x.$ For now, we're interested in the local maximums (not local minimums) of $y=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(3x)\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-3$ and subtracting $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}\u2061(3x)\\le -1 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(3x)\\ge 3 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(3x)-5\\ge 3-5 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(3x)-5\\ge -2 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-2$ at its local minimum points.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
